Modernisation summary
=====================

This source has been updated for current 64-bit macOS and Linux systems:

* The Makefiles use the system C compiler, select platform-appropriate crypt
  linking, and no longer pass obsolete Linux linker options on macOS.
* Pointer alignment and pointer-difference calculations are safe on 64-bit
  systems, including Apple Silicon and x86-64 Linux.
* Obsolete or platform-specific interfaces and headers have been replaced
  with current equivalents.  This includes malloc headers, sigpause,
  sys_errlist, vfork usage in the Ident launcher, and non-standard main
  declarations.
* The main listening socket binds without requiring the machine's hostname
  to have a DNS entry.
* The Ident reply path validates message framing and lengths, bounds buffer
  writes, and safely handles failed or malformed replies.
* Several compiler-discovered defects were corrected, including missing
  standard headers, an out-of-bounds title terminator, unsafe timestamp
  aliasing, overlapping string formatting, and format-string hazards.
* Builds create the runtime directory tree and initial banish files that
  were absent because empty directories were not retained in the archive.
* Generated binaries, object files, logs, and runtime state are excluded by
  the repository .gitignore.

Verified behaviour
==================

* A clean build completes with Apple Clang 17 on arm64 macOS 15 and produces
  native arm64 summink, angel, ident, and intercom executables.
* The coding server completes first-run directory and data initialisation,
  loads its rooms, notes, parser, and help system, and reaches the listening
  state on port 4040 on macOS.
* The build has been exercised with modern GCC on Ubuntu through the source
  files that previously failed because of hidden legacy declarations.  The
  Linux Makefile uses libcrypt; install its development package if linking
  fails.
* A first-login crash was traced to the legacy Ident reply handler.  That
  handler is now bounded and validates replies.  A fresh Ubuntu runtime test
  after this fix is recommended before exposing the service publicly.

The code still produces warnings from its historical C style.  Successful
compilation does not imply that this Internet-facing 1990s server has had a
complete security audit; run it on a private interface while evaluating it.
